## 1. Threat Model & The Cloud-Native Attack Surface

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                               ATTACK VECTORS & FLOW                               |
|                                                                                   |
|  [ Attacker ] ---> ( Initial Access )                                             |
|                    - Phishing, exposed dashboards, CVE in web app, leaked token   |
|                            |                                                      |
|                            v                                                      |
|                    ( Privilege Escalation )                                       |
|                    - RBAC abuse, overly permissive SA token, hostPath breakout   |
|                            |                                                      |
|                            v                                                      |
|                    ( Lateral Movement )                                           |
|                    - Unrestricted Pod-to-Pod traffic, node compromise             |
|                            |                                                      |
|            +---------------+---------------+------------------+                   |
|            |                               |                  |                   |
|            v                               v                  v                   |
|   [ Control Plane Hijack ]       [ Data Exfiltration ]   [ Cryptojacking / ]      |
|   (etcd access, API Admin)       (Secrets, DB Dumps)     [ Workload Wipeout]      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### The Shared Responsibility Model
* **Cloud Provider (AWS EKS / GCP GKE / Azure AKS):** Secures the physical infrastructure, hypervisors, control plane availability, and managed `etcd` hosting.
* **Platform & Security Engineers:** Secure cluster access (OIDC/RBAC), workload configurations (`securityContext`), network isolation (`NetworkPolicies`), runtime detection (Falco), and secret lifecycle.

---

## 2. Authentication (AuthN) & Identity Architecture

```
[ Human User ] ───> [ OIDC Provider (Okta / Azure AD) ] ───> [ Short-Lived JWT ]
                                                                     │
                                                                     ▼
[ kubectl CLI ] ──────────────────────────────────────────> [ kube-apiserver ]
                                                                     │ (Validates Signature)
                                                                     ▼
                                                         [ Authorizes via RBAC ]
```

### Human vs. Workload Authentication
1. **Human Operators:** Kubernetes does **not** store human user accounts natively. All human authentication must integrate with external **OpenID Connect (OIDC)** providers using MFA and short-lived tokens.
2. **In-Cluster Workloads:** Pods authenticate to the API Server using **`ServiceAccounts`** with bound projected volume tokens.

---

## 3. Authorization (AuthZ) & Production RBAC Design

```
                 +---------------------------------+
                 |      Subject (Who)              | (User, Group, ServiceAccount)
                 +----------------+----------------+
                                  |
                                  ▼
                 +---------------------------------+
                 | Role / ClusterRole (What)       | (apiGroups + resources + verbs)
                 +----------------+----------------+
                                  | (Bound via RoleBinding / ClusterRoleBinding)
                                  ▼
                 +---------------------------------+
                 | Target API Object (Where)       | (Namespace or Cluster Scope)
                 +---------------------------------+
```

### Production RBAC Anti-Patterns
1. **Wildcard Verbs / Resources:** Never grant `verbs: ["*"]` or `resources: ["*"]` in production roles.
2. **Global Admin Overuse:** Binding `cluster-admin` to regular developer ServiceAccounts.
3. **`system:authenticated` Abuse:** Binding roles to all authenticated users indiscriminately.

---

## 4. Admission Controllers & Policy-as-Code

```
                     KUBERNETES API REQUEST LIFECYCLE
  HTTP Request ──> AuthN ──> AuthZ (RBAC) ──> Mutating Webhooks ──> Schema Validation ──> Validating Webhooks ──> etcd
```

### Kyverno vs. OPA Gatekeeper
| Feature | Kyverno | OPA Gatekeeper |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Policy Language** | Native Kubernetes YAML | Rego (Declarative query language) |
| **Learning Curve** | Low (Standard K8s manifests) | Medium / High |
| **Capabilities** | Validate, Mutate, Generate, Verify Signatures | Validate, Mutate |
| **Best For** | K8s-native platform teams | Complex enterprise multi-cloud policy |

---

## 5. Pod Security Standards (PSS) & Hardened SecurityContext

### Pod Security Admission (PSA) Levels
* **`Privileged`:** Unrestricted execution (system components and CNI plugins only).
* **`Baseline`:** Blocks known privilege escalations with minimal friction.
* **`Restricted`:** Hardened zero-trust standard (enforces non-root, read-only root FS, drops capabilities).

```bash
# Label namespace for Restricted Pod Security Admission
kubectl label namespace production \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted
```

### Production-Grade Hardened `securityContext`
```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: hardened-app
  namespace: production
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 10001
    runAsGroup: 10001
    fsGroup: 10001
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: registry.company.com/web@sha256:4f8a2b5e...
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      privileged: false
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp-dir
      mountPath: /tmp
  volumes:
  - name: tmp-dir
    emptyDir:
      medium: Memory
```

